(Thermodynamics investigates the macroscopic transformations of heat, work, and internal energy in physical systems. A) Tj
T* (thermodynamic system is characterized by macroscopic coordinates: Pressure P, Volume V, and absolute Temperature T, linked for) Tj
T* (an ideal gas by the equation of state PV = nRT. Internal energy U represents the sum of microscopic kinetic and potential energies) Tj
T* (of all molecules. In an ideal gas with zero intermolecular potential energy, Joule’s law dictates that internal energy depends) Tj
T* (exclusively on temperature: U = U\(T\). When a gas expands by differential volume dV against an external pressure P, the) Tj
T* (mechanical work done by the system is dW = P dV. The First Law of Thermodynamics formulates the conservation of energy: the) Tj
T* (infinitesimal heat dQ absorbed by a system is apportioned between increasing its internal energy dU and performing mechanical) Tj
T* (work dW on its surroundings: dQ = dU + dW = dU + P dV. Under the standard physics sign convention: work done BY the gas) Tj
T* (\(expansion\) is positive \(W > 0\), work done ON the gas \(compression\) is negative \(W < 0\), heat absorbed is positive \(Q > 0\), and) Tj
T* (␀h␀e␀a␀t␀ ␀r␀e␀l␀e␀a␀s␀e␀d␀ ␀i␀s␀ ␀n␀e␀g␀a␀t␀i␀v␀e␀ ␀\(␀Q␀ ␀<␀ ␀0␀\)␀.␀ ␀B␀e␀c␀a␀u␀s␀e␀ ␀U␀ ␀i␀s␀ ␀a␀n␀ ␀e␀x␀a␀c␀t␀ ␀d␀i␀f␀f␀e␀r␀e␀n␀t␀i␀a␀l␀ ␀\(␀s␀t␀a␀t␀e␀ ␀f␀u␀n␀c␀t␀i␀o␀n␀\)␀,␀ ␀i␀t␀s␀ ␀c␀y␀c␀l␀i␀c␀ ␀i␀n␀t␀e␀g␀r␀a␀l␀ ␀i␀s␀ ␀i␀d␀e␀n␀t␀i␀c␀a␀l␀l␀y␀ ␀z␀e␀r␀o␀:␀ ".␀ ␀d␀U␀ ␀=␀ ␀0␀.) Tj
T* (By contrast, heat Q and work W are path-dependent quantities represented by inexact differentials.) Tj
