(2. The Four Fundamental Thermodynamic Processes & P-V Indicator Analysis [12:30 - 19:15]) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
9.7749999999999986 TL
0.078 g
51.0236220472441033 385.5953149606298211 Td
(A thermodynamic process represents the progression of a system from an initial equilibrium state to a final equilibrium state along a) Tj
T* (defined thermodynamic path. Four classical quasi-static processes govern ideal gas behavior: \(1\) Isothermal Process \(T = constant,) Tj
T* (Delta T = 0\): Because internal energy depends solely on temperature, Delta U = 0, which implies Q = W. The process follows) Tj
T* (Boyle’s law \(P V = constant\). On a P-V diagram, it forms an equilateral hyperbola with slope \(dP/dV\)_iso = -P/V. \(2\) Adiabatic) Tj
T* (Process \(Q = 0\): The system is thermally insulated from its surroundings \(or the expansion/compression occurs so rapidly that heat) Tj
T* (exchange is negligible\). From the First Law, W = -Delta U: work done during adiabatic expansion is performed entirely at the) Tj
T* (expense of internal energy, causing a substantial temperature drop. The process is governed by Poisson’s relations: P V^gamma =) Tj
T* (const, T V^\(gamma-1\) = const, and T^gamma P^\(1-gamma\) = const, where gamma = Cp/Cv > 1. The slope on a P-V diagram is) Tj
T* (\(dP/dV\)_adi = -gamma \(P/V\) = gamma x \(dP/dV\)_iso, proving that adiabatic curves are always steeper than isothermal curves) Tj
T* (through the same state point. \(3\) Isobaric Process \(P = constant\): The gas expands or contracts under fixed external pressure.) Tj
T* (Work done is W = P\(V2 - V1\) = nR\(T2 - T1\). Heat supplied is Q = n Cp Delta T. \(4\) Isochoric Process \(V = constant, dV = 0\):) Tj
T* (Because boundaries do not displace, boundary work is identically zero: W = 0. All absorbed heat increases the internal energy of) Tj
T* (the gas: Q = Delta U = n Cv Delta T.) Tj
