(3. Heat Engines, Energy Flow & The Second Law of Thermodynamics [19:15 - 26:00]) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
9.7749999999999986 TL
0.078 g
51.0236220472441033 150.3197244094486678 Td
(A heat engine is a cyclic device that converts thermal energy into continuous mechanical work. It operates between two thermal) Tj
T* (reservoirs: a heat Source maintained at a constant high temperature Th and a heat Sink maintained at a constant low temperature) Tj
T* (Tc \(Th > Tc\). In each operational cycle: \(a\) the working substance absorbs thermal energy Qh from the hot source; \(b\) the working) Tj
T* (substance expands, performing external mechanical work W; \(c\) the working substance rejects residual waste heat Qc into the cold) Tj
T* (sink; \(d\) the working substance is returned to its initial thermodynamic coordinates \(P1, V1, T1\). Because the engine returns to its) Tj
T* (original state, net internal energy change across one complete cycle is zero: Delta U_cycle = 0. Energy conservation gives the net) Tj
T* (mechanical work delivered: W = Qh - Qc. The thermal efficiency eta is defined as the fraction of absorbed heat converted into) Tj
T* (useful work: eta = W / Qh = \(Qh - Qc\) / Qh = 1 - \(Qc / Qh\). The Second Law of Thermodynamics dictates that thermal efficiency can) Tj
T* (never equal 100%: Kelvin-Planck Statement: It is physically impossible to construct an engine operating in a cycle whose sole effect) Tj
T* (is to extract heat from a single thermal reservoir and convert it completely into work. Clausius Statement: It is impossible to) Tj
T* (construct a cyclic machine whose sole effect is the transfer of heat from a colder body to a hotter body without an external work) Tj
T* (input.) Tj
