(4. The Carnot Cycle, Four Reversible Stages & Carnot’s Theorem [26:00 - 38:00]) Tj
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
9.7749999999999986 TL
0.078 g
51.0236220472441033 710.3624409448818824 Td
(In 1824, Nicolas Léonard Sadi Carnot demonstrated that maximum thermal efficiency is achieved by an idealized, reversible cycle) Tj
T* (operating between two temperature limits Th and Tc. The Carnot Cycle consists of four successive reversible operations plotted on) Tj
T* (an indicator \(P-V\) diagram: Stage 1 \(A -> B\): Reversible Isothermal Expansion at Source temperature Th. The cylinder is placed on) Tj
T* (the hot source. The gas absorbs heat Qh and expands from VA to VB doing work W1 = nR Th ln\(VB / VA\). Stage 2 \(B -> C\):) Tj
T* (Reversible Adiabatic Expansion from Th to Tc. The cylinder is placed on a non-conducting stand. Gas expands thermally insulated) Tj
T* (from VB to VC, doing work W2 = nR \(Th - Tc\) / \(gamma - 1\) while temperature falls from Th to Tc. Stage 3 \(C -> D\): Reversible) Tj
T* (Isothermal Compression at Sink temperature Tc. The cylinder is placed on the cold sink. The gas is compressed from VC to VD,) Tj
T* (rejecting heat Qc = nR Tc ln\(VC / VD\) and doing work W3 = -nR Tc ln\(VC / VD\). Stage 4 \(D -> A\): Reversible Adiabatic) Tj
T* (Compression from Tc to Th. The cylinder is placed on the insulating stand. The gas is compressed from VD to VA, absorbing work) Tj
T* (W4 = -nR \(Th - Tc\) / \(gamma - 1\) and restoring the gas to state A \(Th, VA\). Net mechanical work per cycle is W_net = W1 + W2 +) Tj
T* (W3 + W4 = W1 + W3. Because processes B -> C and D -> A are adiabatic: Th VB^\(gamma-1\) = Tc VC^\(gamma-1\) and Th) Tj
T* (␀V␀A␀^␀\(␀g␀a␀m␀m␀a␀-␀1␀\)␀ ␀=␀ ␀T␀c␀ ␀V␀D␀^␀\(␀g␀a␀m␀m␀a␀-␀1␀\)␀.␀ ␀D␀i␀v␀i␀d␀i␀n␀g␀ ␀t␀h␀e␀s␀e␀ ␀t␀w␀o␀ ␀e␀q␀u␀a␀t␀i␀o␀n␀s␀ ␀g␀i␀v␀e␀s␀ ␀\(␀V␀B␀ ␀/␀ ␀V␀A␀\)␀^␀\(␀g␀a␀m␀m␀a␀-␀1␀\)␀ ␀=␀ ␀\(␀V␀C␀ ␀/␀ ␀V␀D␀\)␀^␀\(␀g␀a␀m␀m␀a␀-␀1␀\)␀ !Ò␀ ␀V␀B␀ ␀/␀ ␀V␀A␀ ␀=) Tj
T* (VC / VD. Substituting this ratio reveals: Qc / Qh = Tc / Th! Thus, the Carnot efficiency depends solely on reservoir absolute) Tj
T* (temperatures: eta_carnot = 1 - \(Tc / Th\). Carnot Theorem: \(1\) No engine operating between two given temperatures can be more) Tj
T* (efficient than a reversible Carnot engine operating between the same two temperatures. \(2\) All reversible engines operating) Tj
T* (between the same two temperatures have identical efficiency, completely independent of the working substance.) Tj
